Upstream work consulted and followed (all Apache-2.0 or MIT):

* Qwen3-TTS (qwen-tts Python package, Qwen3-TTS-12Hz model and tokenizer
  configs/weights) — Apache-2.0, Alibaba Qwen team.
  https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen3-TTS
  qwen3_tts/qtts_talker.h, qtts_codec.h follow
  qwen_tts/core/models/modeling_qwen3_tts.py and
  qwen_tts/core/tokenizer_12hz/modeling_qwen3_tts_tokenizer_v2.py.
* Hugging Face transformers (Wav2Vec2 modeling, Qwen2 tokenizer behaviour) — Apache-2.0.
  ja_align/w2v2.h follows Wav2Vec2FeatureEncoder / Wav2Vec2EncoderStableLayerNorm.
* hiragana-asr (DualCTCModel, kana/phoneme vocabularies) and the
  sakasegawa/japanese-wav2vec2-large-hiragana-ctc checkpoint — Apache-2.0.
  https://github.com/nyosegawa/hiragana-asr
  Base model reazon-research/japanese-wav2vec2-large — Apache-2.0.
  Training data ReazonSpeech is CDLA-Sharing-1.0; CDLA places no restriction
  on model outputs. We ship conversion scripts only.
* ctc-segmentation (Kürzinger et al., 2020) — Apache-2.0.
* Hugging Face transformers Mimi (MimiModel encoder path) — Apache-2.0;
  qwen3_tts/qtts_codec_enc.h follows it. qwen3_tts/qtts_spk.h follows the
  ECAPA-TDNN Qwen3TTSSpeakerEncoder in qwen_tts (Apache-2.0).

Evaluation-only tools and data (never linked or redistributed): pyopenjtalk
(accent/reading oracle), SpeechBrain spkrec-ecapa-voxceleb (Apache-2.0, speaker
similarity), JSUT corpus (human baseline / reference voices; research terms).
* Repository components reused (MIT unless noted): common/safetensors.h,
  common/bpe_tokenizer.h + common/unicode_data_tables.inc (derived from
  llama.cpp, MIT; Unicode data under the Unicode license), common/gguf_loader.h,
  common/npy_io.h, common/philox_rng.h, cuda/cuew.{c,h} (Apache-2.0, from Blender),
  cuda/cuda_runner_common.h.

Clean-room components (implemented from papers / specifications only; the
corresponding permissive-but-non-Apache/MIT tools are used solely as
black-box validation oracles, their source was not consulted):

* CTC Viterbi forced alignment (Graves et al., 2006 trellis) — validated against
  torchaudio.functional.forced_align (BSD-2) as an oracle.
* YIN F0 estimation (de Cheveigné & Kawahara, 2002).
* Speaker-encoder log-mel front end (radix-2 FFT, periodic Hann, reflect padding,
  Slaney-scale mel filterbank with Slaney normalization) — torch.stft and
  librosa.filters.mel (ISC) used only as numeric oracles.
* Windowed-sinc resampler, RIFF/WAVE I/O, FFT.
* Japanese kana -> phoneme and phoneme -> viseme tables, written from Japanese
  phonology (label strings follow the Apache-2.0 model vocabulary).
